1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
The space shuttle. It is the most complex machine ever built by man.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:12,000
A triumph of human ingenuity and a testament to the commitment to continued space exploration.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Hi, I'm Story Musgrave. I'm standing at the Kennedy Space Center in Florida,

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
launching pad for every major U.S. space mission in the last five decades

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
and an epicenter of the NASA space program.

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Being the only astronaut to have flown on all five shuttles,

7
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:30,600
I can personally attest to their unique power and beauty.

8
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:33,800
Join me now as we explore mankind's first attempt

9
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:36,740
at a reusable spaceship on modern marvels.

10
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:41,040
Space Shuttle, next on Space Week.

11
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:43,920
Throughout history, the wondrous appeal of flight

12
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:47,880
has held a firm grip on man's imagination.

13
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:51,060
There are those who have attempted to approximate wings

14
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:54,020
and let their spirits soar.

15
00:00:54,021 --> 00:00:56,920
These early efforts were resounding failures.

16
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:12,200
Yet in the first half of the 20th century,

17
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,140
a fearless generation of amateur inventors

18
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:17,140
tried to master the challenge of flight.

19
00:01:17,141 --> 00:01:17,841
of flight.

20
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:26,880
In 1903, two brothers tinkering in the back of their bicycle

21
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:30,120
repair shop strapped a 12 horsepower engine

22
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:33,760
on a simple glider and achieved sustained powered flight.

23
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:37,220
Orville Wright's first voyage lasted only 12 seconds,

24
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:40,760
spanning a mere 120 feet, shorter than the wingspan

25
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:43,140
of most modern commercial planes.

26
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:44,640
But together, the Wright brothers

27
00:01:44,641 --> 00:01:47,340
lifted the world's perception of the possible

28
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:48,940
and cracked open the skies.

29
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:57,680
But while the public applauded the accomplishments

30
00:01:57,780 --> 00:02:00,660
of aviation's first heroes, scientists

31
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:03,400
were already making important discoveries, which

32
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:05,820
would change the nature of powered flight

33
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:09,600
and propel the world into an uncertain future.

34
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:12,460
March 16, 1926, more than a year

35
00:02:12,461 --> 00:02:15,360
before Lindbergh's solo flight across the Atlantic,

36
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:18,460
Dr. Robert Goddard designs, builds, and fires

37
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:21,900
the world's first liquid-fueled rocket in the desert

38
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:23,500
of Roswell, New Mexico.

39
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:30,820
These rare home movies illustrate the early potential

40
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:33,220
of his revolutionary new concept,

41
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:34,920
a fundamentally different approach

42
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:37,960
to propulsion with an internal combustion engine

43
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:41,860
that isn't based on a mechanical model.

44
00:02:41,861 --> 00:02:44,760
Dr. Goddard based his work on careful calculations,

45
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:48,440
but often alienated his peers by suggesting that rockets would

46
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:53,200
make a trip to the moon a reality.

47
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:55,500
At his Roswell test site in the 1930s,

48
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:59,620
Goddard and his staff built and tested a rocket a month.

49
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:01,620
They came in all shapes and sizes,

50
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:05,120
carrying scientific equipment and rising from the desert floor

51
00:03:05,220 --> 00:03:08,380
at speeds up to 600 miles per hour.

52
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:11,160
Goddard's home movies also reveal another space age

53
00:03:11,161 --> 00:03:13,860
innovation, recovery by parachute.

54
00:03:16,660 --> 00:03:19,900
Few Americans realize the implications of Goddard's work

55
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,400
in his rocket research was greatly ignored.

56
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:25,100
But in Nazi Germany during the 1930s,

57
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:29,140
scientists like Werner von Braun devoured Goddard's theories

58
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:32,520
and gave the rocket its first practical application,

59
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:35,380
the V-2 bomb.

60
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:37,460
Constructed in exactly the same manner

61
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:39,520
as Dr. Goddard's New Mexico rockets,

62
00:03:39,521 --> 00:03:42,620
the V2s delivered explosive death to the British,

63
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:46,360
destroying homes, businesses, great cathedrals,

64
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:49,700
and the civilians inside.

65
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:53,100
After the war, German scientists were interrogated

66
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:55,040
about the design of their missiles.

67
00:03:55,140 --> 00:03:57,000
Why are you asking us, they replied?

68
00:03:57,100 --> 00:04:01,100
Why don't you ask your own rocket pioneer, Dr. Goddard?

69
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:04,280
We learned these things from him.

70
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:08,040
Unfortunately, Dr. Goddard had died in August of 1945,

71
00:04:08,041 --> 00:04:11,380
just as the world was reeling from an explosive new future

72
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:13,680
shock.

73
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:16,180
Rockets facilitated the nuclear age.

74
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:19,880
After all, if a nation were to use such a destructive weapon,

75
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:23,920
it would be important to pull the trigger from very far away.

76
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:26,800
Rockets give warriors a comfortable distance

77
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:30,100
from the destruction they unleash.

78
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,440
But in the Cold War years that followed,

79
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:34,540
the United States and the Soviet Union

80
00:04:34,541 --> 00:04:37,880
competed to be able to put a far more innovative payload

81
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:39,980
into a missile.

82
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:42,980
And the race to lay claim to outer space began.

83
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:53,900
In 1954, white mice were sealed in a rocket nose cone

84
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,400
for an experimental flight.

85
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:02,000
This was the first physical evidence

86
00:05:02,001 --> 00:05:05,600
that living organisms could survive in a zero gravity

87
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:07,800
environment.

88
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:11,440
Meanwhile, on Earth, John Paul Stapp rides a rocket sled

89
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:14,820
at supersonic speeds to see if the human body can withstand

90
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:17,920
the grueling effects of space flight.

91
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:20,020
His face reflecting the strain, Stapp

92
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:24,620
is subjected to pressure 35 times his own weight as his sled

93
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:25,720
is braked to a stop.

94
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,700
But while the United States set an agenda of controlled

95
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:38,600
scientific advancement, Russia leaps ahead

96
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:42,280
with an astounding demonstration of bravado.

97
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:46,320
October 4, 1957, the world listens to the faint chirp

98
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:50,580
of Sputnik 1, the first man-made satellite circling the globe

99
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:54,120
every 90 minutes, and the race escalates.

100
00:05:54,121 --> 00:05:54,821
escalates.

101
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:04,360
In America, a chimpanzee named Ham is wired

102
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:07,960
with delicate electronic sensors, then packed in a rocket

103
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:12,340
and blasted 150 miles into the stratosphere.

104
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:15,100
Minutes later, he's recovered by Navy helicopters

105
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:20,220
in the Atlantic, 450 miles from the launch site.

106
00:06:20,221 --> 00:06:24,420
Russia responds with Laika, a puppy sent into complete Earth

107
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:26,960
orbit aboard Sputnik 2.

108
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:29,560
Unlike the American chimp, Laika

109
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:31,660
was allowed to burn up upon reentry.

110
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:37,980
In 1959, America recruits military test pilots

111
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:39,680
as future astronauts.

112
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:42,680
But before their training is complete, Russia scores again.

113
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:48,320
April 1961, Moscow and the world

114
00:06:48,321 --> 00:06:51,060
hail Yuri Gagarin after the Russian cosmonaut

115
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:56,000
becomes the first human being to orbit the Earth.

116
00:06:56,100 --> 00:06:59,100
America must respond, and President Kennedy

117
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:01,700
charts the course.

118
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:04,540
We choose to go to the moon.

119
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:06,040
We choose to go to the moon.

120
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:15,380
We choose to go to the moon in this decade

121
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:18,080
and do the other thing, not because they

122
00:07:18,081 --> 00:07:20,920
they are easy, but because they are hard.

123
00:07:20,921 --> 00:07:24,120
I believe that this nation should commit itself

124
00:07:24,121 --> 00:07:28,360
to achieving the goal before this decade is out

125
00:07:28,361 --> 00:07:31,520
of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely

126
00:07:31,521 --> 00:07:33,360
to the earth.

127
00:07:33,361 --> 00:07:36,440
No single space project in this period

128
00:07:36,441 --> 00:07:40,000
will be more impressive to mankind or more important

129
00:07:40,001 --> 00:07:42,400
for the long-range exploration of space.

130
00:07:42,401 --> 00:07:45,400
One, zero, ignition.

131
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:52,680
February 1962, John Glenn orbits the Earth,

132
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:56,320
and America holds its breath as a new frontier beacons

133
00:07:56,420 --> 00:07:58,920
the nation towards unknown worlds.

134
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:00,220
This is Friendship 7.

135
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:03,300
I can see clear back a big cloud pattern way back

136
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:05,300
across toward the Cape.

137
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:07,740
Beautiful sight.

138
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:10,140
Not all rockets work to perfection,

139
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:13,840
but while NASA understood that progress is built on failure

140
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:16,680
as much as success, the Soviet Union seemed

141
00:08:16,681 --> 00:08:19,440
The Soviet Union seemed to flaunt its impressive string

142
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:23,880
of accomplishments, including the first woman in space,

143
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:31,300
Valentina Tereshkova, who orbited the globe in June of 1963.

144
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:34,960
NASA called her a Soviet Barbie doll who was only long for the

145
00:08:35,060 --> 00:08:36,260
ride.

146
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:39,260
But a Russian space agency official boasted that she was

147
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:42,440
a brilliant star in the cosmic firmament.

148
00:08:42,540 --> 00:08:46,680
Never before and in no country did women ever obtain such height.

149
00:08:46,681 --> 00:08:49,380
be another 20 years before the first American woman would

150
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:51,080
experience outer space.

151
00:08:55,980 --> 00:08:59,920
The dark mysteries of the cosmos and the extraordinary perspective

152
00:09:00,020 --> 00:09:03,160
of Earth from orbit seemed to offer another motivation

153
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:05,400
for the American space program that

154
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:08,140
transcended the technical rivalries of two

155
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:10,780
Earth-bound superpowers.

156
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,800
Suddenly, the world began to realize

157
00:09:12,900 --> 00:09:15,400
that the immediate goal of landing on the moon

158
00:09:15,401 --> 00:09:20,140
was part of a more profound journey for humankind.

159
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:24,440
When two men walked on the lunar surface, so did we all.

160
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:32,080
When humankind claimed the moon in 1969,

161
00:09:32,180 --> 00:09:37,120
the Earth was a landscape of war and epic social revolution.

162
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:40,220
But in space, humankind's most noble dreams

163
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:43,720
came into crystal focus.

164
00:09:43,721 --> 00:09:47,160
Those few fortunate souls who experienced outer space

165
00:09:47,260 --> 00:09:50,360
understood that there was hope in the heavens,

166
00:09:50,460 --> 00:09:53,460
that human beings need a relationship with the stars

167
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:57,140
that feeds curiosity instead of competition,

168
00:09:57,240 --> 00:09:59,840
a way must be found to shuttle more people

169
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:01,300
into the province of space.

170
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:11,740
Well, then why we got a problem.

171
00:10:11,741 --> 00:10:14,280
Put up chart number four, please.

172
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:17,040
Do you need this small segment called the tunnel segment

173
00:10:17,140 --> 00:10:20,380
if you are not going into space last?

174
00:10:20,480 --> 00:10:23,880
Go to chart 2489A.

175
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:26,980
These men and women are doing the impossible.

176
00:10:27,080 --> 00:10:30,200
They are creating humankind's very first reusable

177
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:32,100
extraterrestrial spaceship.

178
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:33,900
And what if you cut the interface here,

179
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
and you don't need something on the street?

180
00:10:36,100 --> 00:10:38,500
Well, then why can't I go EVA, too, with 5,000?

181
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:39,900
You can go out here.

182
00:10:39,901 --> 00:10:43,700
Let's go to chart number seven, please.

183
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:47,900
There will be no prefabricated parts, no instruction book.

184
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,920
Every element of the project is brand new,

185
00:10:51,020 --> 00:10:54,840
poised on the cutting edge of science and imagination.

186
00:11:02,260 --> 00:11:03,320
17 Houston, you are in.

187
00:11:03,420 --> 00:11:04,220
Go for orbit.

188
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:06,620
Go for orbit.

189
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:08,760
It had always been NASA's intent

190
00:11:08,761 --> 00:11:12,000
to create a method of transporting men and materials

191
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:14,400
between Earth and the heavens.

192
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:17,000
But the project accelerated after the conclusion

193
00:11:17,100 --> 00:11:22,600
of the Apollo moon missions in the early 1970s.

194
00:11:22,700 --> 00:11:25,580
In many ways, the technology behind a space shuttle

195
00:11:25,680 --> 00:11:29,520
is more sophisticated than a lunar expedition.

196
00:11:29,620 --> 00:11:31,880
In both concept and completion, it

197
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:34,520
is an unprecedented human achievement,

198
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:36,680
designed to rocket into Earth orbit

199
00:11:36,681 --> 00:11:41,180
at a height of 350 miles, carry up to seven people,

200
00:11:41,280 --> 00:11:45,560
and 65,000 pounds of freight, perform delicate missions

201
00:11:45,660 --> 00:11:49,520
in zero gravity, then land back on the planet's surface

202
00:11:49,620 --> 00:11:53,800
like a glider and be prepared to repeat the trip 100 times

203
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:54,600
over.

204
00:11:57,260 --> 00:11:59,400
The first practical challenge of the endeavor

205
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:03,380
concerns the vehicle's ability to withstand the 2,500 degree

206
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:06,340
heat of reentry.

207
00:12:06,341 --> 00:12:08,540
When the Apollo astronauts returned from the moon,

208
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:11,580
their command module was protected from incineration

209
00:12:11,680 --> 00:12:14,420
by a heat shield of asbestos resin,

210
00:12:14,520 --> 00:12:17,080
which burned up upon reentry.

211
00:12:17,180 --> 00:12:20,880
But the shuttle shield must not burn away.

212
00:12:20,980 --> 00:12:23,660
Instead, a remarkable new insulating material

213
00:12:23,760 --> 00:12:27,600
was developed, which can be used on up to 100 excursions

214
00:12:27,700 --> 00:12:30,660
to space and back.

215
00:12:30,760 --> 00:12:33,060
With the weight and consistency of styrofoam,

216
00:12:33,061 --> 00:12:37,300
it is manufactured from an easily obtainable source, sand.

217
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:44,580
The heat dissipating properties of the shuttle's insulation

218
00:12:44,680 --> 00:12:48,840
is so efficient, it can be handled while still red hot.

219
00:12:52,820 --> 00:12:55,480
The shuttle's 34,000 insulation tiles

220
00:12:55,580 --> 00:12:58,660
must be individually milled and fitted to their unique place

221
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:02,080
on the vehicle, covering the ship's aluminum skin, which

222
00:13:02,081 --> 00:13:07,160
which would otherwise melt at a mere 320 degrees.

223
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:10,160
This requires that each tile have its own code number, which

224
00:13:10,260 --> 00:13:13,360
relates to the specific place and curvature of the area

225
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:17,020
for which it was cut, then sprayed with a polymer

226
00:13:17,120 --> 00:13:20,460
to avoid absorbing moisture and weight between flights.

227
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:28,580
Like parts of a jigsaw puzzle, shuttle components

228
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:30,500
from contractors across the country

229
00:13:30,501 --> 00:13:33,540
flow into the California Assembly Facility of Rockwell

230
00:13:33,640 --> 00:13:35,800
International.

231
00:13:35,900 --> 00:13:40,780
This wing section has come from Long Island,

232
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:45,620
wheel and brake assemblies from Ohio, flight control system

233
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:50,820
from Florida, payload bay doors from Oklahoma, and crew living

234
00:13:50,920 --> 00:13:53,960
quarters from Colorado.

235
00:13:54,060 --> 00:13:55,760
From all across the United States,

236
00:13:55,860 --> 00:13:58,420
people have worked at solving the shuttle's construction

237
00:13:58,421 --> 00:14:03,260
challenges, from special robotic welding

238
00:14:03,360 --> 00:14:06,220
to using lasers to precisely cut insulation

239
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:07,960
from around electrical wiring.

240
00:14:13,260 --> 00:14:18,180
There are four separate propulsion systems on the shuttle.

241
00:14:18,280 --> 00:14:21,640
Twin rocket boosters support the orbiter and the external tank

242
00:14:21,740 --> 00:14:25,020
on the launch pad and supply most of the thrust for blast

243
00:14:25,021 --> 00:14:27,920
and the first two minutes of flight.

244
00:14:28,020 --> 00:14:30,720
The main propulsion system, a cluster of three liquid

245
00:14:30,820 --> 00:14:33,660
propellant rocket engines supplied with liquid oxygen

246
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:35,760
and liquid hydrogen from the external tank.

247
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:43,100
The orbital maneuvering system, two pods mounted on the aft,

248
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:46,100
producing propulsion for the final stage of ascent,

249
00:14:46,200 --> 00:14:49,440
maneuvering in space, and retrofiring for descent.

250
00:14:49,441 --> 00:14:56,080
The reaction control system comprised of 44 small rockets

251
00:14:56,180 --> 00:14:58,420
mounted in clusters in the nose section

252
00:14:58,520 --> 00:15:01,820
and in the two rear pods, which align the shuttle

253
00:15:01,920 --> 00:15:02,920
while in orbit.

254
00:15:09,420 --> 00:15:12,160
The solid rocket boosters are the largest of their kind

255
00:15:12,260 --> 00:15:17,100
ever built, 150 feet long, 12 feet in diameter,

256
00:15:17,101 --> 00:15:21,840
assembled from four segments of 1 1⁄2 inch steel.

257
00:15:21,940 --> 00:15:25,580
The solid propellant inside the casing looks and feels

258
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:30,580
like the hard rubber of a typewriter eraser.

259
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:33,780
After ignition, the boosters will be up to full operating

260
00:15:33,880 --> 00:15:37,220
pressure in less than one second, with a combined thrust

261
00:15:37,320 --> 00:15:39,020
of nearly 3 million pounds.

262
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:45,620
The three main engines can swivel 21 degrees up and down

263
00:15:45,621 --> 00:15:47,860
up and down in the vertical position,

264
00:15:47,960 --> 00:15:51,560
and 17 degrees from side to side to steer the vehicle

265
00:15:51,660 --> 00:15:53,560
during flight.

266
00:15:53,660 --> 00:15:56,660
The liquid hydrogen and oxygen use a propellant stored

267
00:15:56,760 --> 00:16:00,940
in the external tank, a structure just over 150 feet

268
00:16:01,040 --> 00:16:05,880
long with a diameter of 28 feet, constructed of aluminum alloy

269
00:16:05,980 --> 00:16:07,380
up to two inches thick.

270
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:12,520
It is actually two propellant tanks

271
00:16:12,521 --> 00:16:17,100
connected by a cylindrical collar called an inner tank.

272
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:21,500
The forward tank holds 140,000 gallons of liquid oxygen

273
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:25,960
at minus 297 degrees Fahrenheit.

274
00:16:26,060 --> 00:16:30,280
The aft tank holds 380,000 gallons of liquid hydrogen

275
00:16:30,380 --> 00:16:34,580
at minus 420 degrees.

276
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:36,680
Before assembling the final craft,

277
00:16:36,780 --> 00:16:39,680
45 tests at six different facilities

278
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:42,380
were performed to make certain the propulsion

279
00:16:42,381 --> 00:16:43,620
operated perfectly.

280
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:55,520
But while the mechanical elements of the craft

281
00:16:55,620 --> 00:16:57,820
were being constructed and analyzed,

282
00:16:57,920 --> 00:16:59,740
the human element was being prepared

283
00:16:59,840 --> 00:17:03,740
inside the most elaborate testing facility ever conceived.

284
00:17:03,741 --> 00:17:13,080
The space shuttle represents the combined investments of three

285
00:17:13,180 --> 00:17:18,260
decades in space, a generation of scientists and technicians,

286
00:17:18,360 --> 00:17:21,160
and billions of dollars in hardware.

287
00:17:21,260 --> 00:17:23,620
It is important that whoever is finally

288
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:26,700
chosen to represent humankind on a shuttle mission

289
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:28,940
be equipped with intensive training

290
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:33,740
in order to address any known or unknown problem in space.

291
00:17:33,741 --> 00:17:36,240
To accomplish this goal, scientists

292
00:17:36,340 --> 00:17:39,940
developed the most highly advanced computerized virtual

293
00:17:40,040 --> 00:17:45,280
reality device ever created, the SMS.

294
00:17:45,380 --> 00:17:48,180
Well, the SMS stands for Shuttle Mission Simulator.

295
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:49,680
That's exactly what it is.

296
00:17:49,780 --> 00:17:51,900
It simulates the shuttle mission.

297
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,700
We can simulate ascent, on orbit, and entry.

298
00:17:55,800 --> 00:17:59,400
We've got all the switches that allow us to do that.

299
00:17:59,500 --> 00:18:01,840
This exact high-fidelity replica

300
00:18:01,841 --> 00:18:04,000
replica of the shuttle's crew station

301
00:18:04,100 --> 00:18:06,640
has seating for the commander, pilot, and two mission

302
00:18:06,740 --> 00:18:09,280
specialists to enact team training

303
00:18:09,380 --> 00:18:12,680
in an ultra-realistic environment.

304
00:18:12,780 --> 00:18:15,980
100 gears down and locked.

305
00:18:16,080 --> 00:18:19,060
All controls and displays in the motion bay simulator

306
00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:22,820
are active with visual and audio cues provided

307
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:25,400
for enhancement of the simulation.

308
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:27,700
The simulator takes a little getting used to, especially

309
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:29,040
the motion bay.

310
00:18:29,140 --> 00:18:31,040
After a few hundred hours in the simulator, though,

311
00:18:31,041 --> 00:18:32,840
you're fairly comfortable in there,

312
00:18:32,940 --> 00:18:35,580
and it feels just like you're at home.

313
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:36,780
That's exactly what you want.

314
00:18:36,880 --> 00:18:38,040
You want to be very comfortable when you

315
00:18:38,140 --> 00:18:39,040
strap into the spacecraft.

316
00:18:42,660 --> 00:18:45,580
The crew station is mounted on a platform and tilt frame

317
00:18:45,680 --> 00:18:49,660
assembly with six independent servo actuators, which

318
00:18:49,760 --> 00:18:52,660
create cabin movements similar to what would be experienced

319
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:54,360
on a mission.

320
00:18:54,460 --> 00:18:56,340
Outside the motion-based simulator

321
00:18:56,440 --> 00:18:58,600
is an operator station where technicians

322
00:18:58,700 --> 00:19:00,740
prepare the training sessions and monitor

323
00:19:00,741 --> 00:19:03,640
the crew's behavior.

324
00:19:03,740 --> 00:19:05,140
It's kind of funny.

325
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:06,840
When you get involved in a simulation,

326
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:08,540
you kind of forget that it's a simulation.

327
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:10,580
You tend to mentally think it's a real thing.

328
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:11,580
And you're thinking about it.

329
00:19:11,680 --> 00:19:13,980
Your palms start to sweat, and you get nervous, just like it

330
00:19:14,080 --> 00:19:16,280
was a real thing.

331
00:19:16,380 --> 00:19:19,100
An advanced digital image generation program

332
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:22,600
creates the high-fidelity window scenes,

333
00:19:22,700 --> 00:19:25,260
processing and displaying three-dimensional scenes

334
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:28,740
for all windows of the crew station.

335
00:19:28,741 --> 00:19:33,740
A team of instructors is assigned to each flight crew.

336
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:37,480
Ironically, instructors help improve performance

337
00:19:37,580 --> 00:19:42,280
by causing in-flight emergencies.

338
00:19:42,380 --> 00:19:45,520
Interactive controls are used to insert malfunctions

339
00:19:45,620 --> 00:19:48,020
and monitor system status.

340
00:19:48,120 --> 00:19:51,800
There are approximately 6,800 simulated malfunctions,

341
00:19:51,900 --> 00:19:56,400
which can be activated either manually or automatically.

342
00:19:56,401 --> 00:19:58,160
A script is written to give direction

343
00:19:58,260 --> 00:19:59,500
to a simulated mission.

344
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:07,040
If it requires some coordination of malfunctions,

345
00:20:07,140 --> 00:20:12,080
we'll have a meeting, say, two or three days before the session.

346
00:20:12,180 --> 00:20:14,480
I'll outline what the lesson is.

347
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:18,060
And then from there, I'll let the individual instructors

348
00:20:18,160 --> 00:20:21,020
then start adding their inputs into the malfunctions that they

349
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:22,320
like on each of those cases.

350
00:20:22,420 --> 00:20:25,100
And we'll outline each run in terms of each malfunction

351
00:20:25,101 --> 00:20:26,340
that's going to go in on that run.

352
00:20:26,440 --> 00:20:29,000
And we'll give it a time when that malfunction is going in

353
00:20:29,100 --> 00:20:31,540
and then the impact that malfunction will have.

354
00:20:31,640 --> 00:20:33,580
And we'll end up with a script package

355
00:20:33,680 --> 00:20:36,540
for a particular lesson, which will

356
00:20:36,640 --> 00:20:38,640
have all the different runs in it.

357
00:20:38,740 --> 00:20:42,220
I try to make sure that they get a well-rounded training

358
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:45,220
and from each lesson to lesson to make sure that we've

359
00:20:45,320 --> 00:20:48,160
covered all the objectives that we can.

360
00:20:48,260 --> 00:20:50,920
Can't go to SM because we got that one on SM.

361
00:20:51,020 --> 00:20:53,320
Approximately 10 weeks prior to launch,

362
00:20:53,321 --> 00:20:57,020
the crew begins the final flight-specific training.

363
00:20:57,120 --> 00:21:01,960
Bypass, ISO circuit breaker on L4.

364
00:21:02,060 --> 00:21:05,860
Columbia, Houston, Energy, ground track and nav, go.

365
00:21:05,960 --> 00:21:08,160
Roger, Houston.

366
00:21:08,260 --> 00:21:10,540
Instructors apply a carrot and stick approach

367
00:21:10,640 --> 00:21:12,380
to the training dynamic.

368
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:15,200
But the team's spirit keeps the necessary instructional

369
00:21:15,300 --> 00:21:19,140
sabotage in good-natured perspective.

370
00:21:19,240 --> 00:21:22,020
We started off a long time ago joking about the stick

371
00:21:22,021 --> 00:21:26,740
stick and the carrot approach where if somebody goose up and doesn't run the malfunction right

372
00:21:26,741 --> 00:21:30,380
you either beat them with a stick or hold a carrot out in front of them if they did it right.

373
00:21:30,740 --> 00:21:35,740
So it became a means of rewarding them for something done.

374
00:21:36,080 --> 00:21:40,200
I know one of the guys brought me a carrot that's this big around that he grew in his garden

375
00:21:40,201 --> 00:21:45,040
out there and nobody's done anything that good yet to earn that carrot.

376
00:21:45,320 --> 00:21:48,374
This is in accordance with the traditions of SMSP making.

377
00:21:48,375 --> 00:21:51,000
I'd like to show our respect and our appreciation

378
00:21:51,001 --> 00:21:53,140
and I did a heck of a job.

379
00:21:53,240 --> 00:21:54,540
I can present these to you.

380
00:21:54,640 --> 00:21:55,740
You should.

381
00:22:00,140 --> 00:22:02,640
Meanwhile, at Cape Canaveral on the eastern shore

382
00:22:02,740 --> 00:22:05,240
of Florida, the final assembly of the shuttle

383
00:22:05,340 --> 00:22:07,760
is quickly coming to a dramatic conclusion.

384
00:22:11,660 --> 00:22:13,960
First, the solid rocket booster segments

385
00:22:14,060 --> 00:22:17,100
are stacked on the mobile launching platform.

386
00:22:17,200 --> 00:22:20,360
Next, the external tank is lifted 350 feet

387
00:22:20,361 --> 00:22:23,660
above the ground and lowered between the boosters

388
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:26,460
in a delicate and demanding six-hour ordeal.

389
00:22:29,160 --> 00:22:32,500
Finally, the shuttle orbiter is rolled to the vehicle assembly

390
00:22:32,600 --> 00:22:37,080
building to be mated to tank and boosters.

391
00:22:37,180 --> 00:22:40,320
A massive handling sling holds the 200-ton orbiter

392
00:22:40,420 --> 00:22:43,980
for a final inspection of the landing gear.

393
00:22:44,080 --> 00:22:46,220
Then the shuttle is lifted from a horizontal

394
00:22:46,320 --> 00:22:49,780
to a vertical position, suspended over the fuel tank

395
00:22:49,781 --> 00:22:53,480
assembly and lowered into place.

396
00:22:53,580 --> 00:22:56,780
This 12-hour operation is the most critical part

397
00:22:56,880 --> 00:23:00,420
of the final assembly process.

398
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:03,520
As the completed shuttle rolls from the assembly building

399
00:23:03,620 --> 00:23:06,440
to the launch pad, the 3 1⁄2-mile crawl

400
00:23:06,540 --> 00:23:10,440
offers the team a moment of reflection before the countdown.

401
00:23:10,441 --> 00:23:11,940
in the hometown.

402
00:23:16,480 --> 00:23:19,520
Sunday, April 12, 1981.

403
00:23:19,620 --> 00:23:23,260
The space shuttle, 14 stories high.

404
00:23:23,360 --> 00:23:26,120
A liftoff weight of 4 and 1⁄2 million pounds.

405
00:23:30,300 --> 00:23:35,160
Columbia, poised on the pad for its maiden flight.

406
00:23:35,260 --> 00:23:38,560
Its 54 and 1⁄2 hour mission to prove the practicality

407
00:23:38,561 --> 00:23:40,700
the space frontier.

408
00:23:40,800 --> 00:23:43,660
An American spaceship has never carried a human crew

409
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:45,360
on its first voyage.

410
00:23:45,460 --> 00:23:47,740
Columbia will leave planet Earth with a pair

411
00:23:47,840 --> 00:23:50,340
of highly trained explorers.

412
00:23:50,440 --> 00:23:53,980
Commander John Young with 533 hours in space.

413
00:23:54,080 --> 00:23:56,120
He is the most experienced astronaut

414
00:23:56,220 --> 00:23:58,520
in the American space program.

415
00:23:58,620 --> 00:24:00,280
Pilot Robert Crippen.

416
00:24:00,380 --> 00:24:02,620
This will be his first experience in space.

417
00:24:08,560 --> 00:24:12,860
2,700 reporters and photographers from around the world

418
00:24:12,960 --> 00:24:15,360
are on hand to record the event.

419
00:24:18,300 --> 00:24:24,400
Over 600,000 spectators surround the Kennedy Space Center.

420
00:24:24,500 --> 00:24:31,420
T minus 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4.

421
00:24:31,520 --> 00:24:33,360
We've gone for main engine start.

422
00:25:15,660 --> 00:25:17,560
Roger roll.

423
00:25:17,660 --> 00:25:21,200
Rolling the shuttle in the first few moments of flight

424
00:25:21,300 --> 00:25:23,900
puts the craft into a precise heading.

425
00:25:32,420 --> 00:25:34,920
40 seconds into flight, the Columbia is too high

426
00:25:34,921 --> 00:25:38,520
is too high for the astronauts to safely use their ejector

427
00:25:38,620 --> 00:25:39,520
seats.

428
00:25:39,620 --> 00:25:43,160
This is the point of no return.

429
00:25:43,260 --> 00:25:44,560
Roger, Columbia, on a nice drive.

430
00:25:44,660 --> 00:25:46,560
You're launching a little bit, but you'll probably be slightly

431
00:25:46,660 --> 00:25:48,160
high at sea.

432
00:25:48,260 --> 00:25:49,060
Negative seats.

433
00:25:49,160 --> 00:25:52,480
Columbia, you're negative seats.

434
00:25:52,580 --> 00:25:55,080
We see SRVS flight.

435
00:25:55,180 --> 00:25:56,480
Roger, on a jet.

436
00:25:56,580 --> 00:25:58,080
Columbia, come on, right, bud.

437
00:25:58,180 --> 00:26:00,680
The fuel expended, the solid rocket boosters

438
00:26:00,780 --> 00:26:02,680
are released and fall back to Earth.

439
00:26:02,780 --> 00:26:04,680
Columbia, you're looking a little hot dog.

440
00:26:04,681 --> 00:26:06,180
I'm calling you a little early.

441
00:26:06,280 --> 00:26:09,180
I'm calling you.

442
00:26:09,280 --> 00:26:11,380
And Columbia, Houston, just for your information,

443
00:26:11,480 --> 00:26:13,580
you dropped those SRVs right on target.

444
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:15,720
And they were floating just the way they ought to be.

445
00:26:15,820 --> 00:26:17,220
And the boats were getting ready to fish them

446
00:26:17,320 --> 00:26:20,120
and bring them back.

447
00:26:20,220 --> 00:26:24,340
The boosters are recovered 150 miles from the launch site.

448
00:26:24,440 --> 00:26:29,540
Towed back to land, they will be refurbished and used again.

449
00:26:29,640 --> 00:26:31,640
On the shuttle, another main engine blast

450
00:26:31,641 --> 00:26:35,540
takes the craft to an altitude of 172 miles,

451
00:26:35,640 --> 00:26:39,240
with 42 million horsepower.

452
00:26:39,340 --> 00:26:41,180
Before the main engine cut off and release,

453
00:26:41,280 --> 00:26:47,080
the ship will be cruising at 18,000 miles per hour.

454
00:26:47,180 --> 00:26:48,600
Columbia, Houston.

455
00:26:48,700 --> 00:26:50,500
You guys did so good, we're going to let you stay up there

456
00:26:50,600 --> 00:26:51,300
for a couple of days.

457
00:26:51,400 --> 00:26:52,200
You're go for on orbit.

458
00:26:56,940 --> 00:26:58,640
This maiden flight of Columbia

459
00:26:58,740 --> 00:27:00,740
demonstrated the space shuttle's ability

460
00:27:00,741 --> 00:27:03,780
to facilitate scientific experiments and satellite

461
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:05,080
maintenance.

462
00:27:05,180 --> 00:27:07,740
Future missions will be able to release high-tech equipment

463
00:27:07,840 --> 00:27:10,120
cheaper and with more accuracy of position

464
00:27:10,220 --> 00:27:13,620
than ever before achieved.

465
00:27:13,720 --> 00:27:16,460
Earth studies will also be enhanced with information

466
00:27:16,560 --> 00:27:20,100
gathered from the unique perspective of space.

467
00:27:20,200 --> 00:27:22,620
500 enhanced images of Earth were captured

468
00:27:22,720 --> 00:27:24,360
on the first flight alone.

469
00:27:24,460 --> 00:27:27,540
Sand dunes 1,500 feet high.

470
00:27:27,541 --> 00:27:32,020
Islands, the soaring height of the Himalayas,

471
00:27:32,120 --> 00:27:36,420
wide expanses in Iran exposed to devastating wind erosion.

472
00:27:41,860 --> 00:27:45,060
Down on the planet's surface, a special team of 24 vehicles

473
00:27:45,160 --> 00:27:47,720
and 100 people prepare to power down Colombia

474
00:27:47,820 --> 00:27:51,140
after its landing in the California desert.

475
00:27:51,240 --> 00:27:53,360
The public is on hand as well.

476
00:27:53,460 --> 00:27:55,500
A string of traffic six miles long

477
00:27:55,501 --> 00:27:58,260
surrounds the base as a half million people come

478
00:27:58,360 --> 00:28:02,340
to witness Columbia's return.

479
00:28:02,440 --> 00:28:05,340
Reentry begins at 17,000 miles per hour,

480
00:28:05,440 --> 00:28:07,840
26 times the speed of sound.

481
00:28:07,940 --> 00:28:12,080
When it touches down, it will be traveling at 216 miles per hour.

482
00:28:12,180 --> 00:28:14,880
But the space shuttle has no propulsion power.

483
00:28:14,980 --> 00:28:17,360
As it glides back into the atmosphere,

484
00:28:17,460 --> 00:28:20,060
it must land on the first attempt.

485
00:28:20,160 --> 00:28:22,160
And John, we're showing you rolling right.

486
00:28:22,260 --> 00:28:23,060
Looking good.

487
00:28:23,061 --> 00:28:23,900
All right, stop.

488
00:28:23,901 --> 00:28:24,720
All right, stop.

489
00:28:24,721 --> 00:28:25,560
All right.

490
00:28:28,560 --> 00:28:32,400
The gear is coming down at 270.

491
00:28:32,401 --> 00:28:34,700
50 feet.

492
00:28:34,701 --> 00:28:36,700
40 feet.

493
00:28:36,701 --> 00:28:38,700
30.

494
00:28:38,701 --> 00:28:41,040
40.

495
00:28:41,041 --> 00:28:42,280
10 knots, 200 knots.

496
00:28:42,281 --> 00:28:43,540
40.

497
00:28:43,541 --> 00:28:44,880
190 knots.

498
00:28:44,881 --> 00:28:46,380
Speed brakes are tracking.

499
00:28:46,381 --> 00:28:47,780
Down.

500
00:28:47,781 --> 00:28:51,580
Fresh from space, mission commander John Young

501
00:28:51,680 --> 00:28:53,880
shared his jubilation.

502
00:28:53,980 --> 00:28:56,520
JOHN YOUNG, I can't tell you what a tribute

503
00:28:56,620 --> 00:28:58,260
that is to the American working man

504
00:28:58,360 --> 00:29:00,320
and the American working woman, too.

505
00:29:00,420 --> 00:29:02,360
You can't imagine the variety of people

506
00:29:02,460 --> 00:29:05,160
who worked on this vehicle from all walks of life,

507
00:29:05,260 --> 00:29:07,640
all capabilities and limitations.

508
00:29:07,740 --> 00:29:10,060
It's all due to their individual efforts.

509
00:29:10,160 --> 00:29:12,000
They proved that they can do the job.

510
00:29:12,100 --> 00:29:14,280
They proved it for the world to see.

511
00:29:14,380 --> 00:29:16,000
And I'm not sure what they did.

512
00:29:16,001 --> 00:29:19,340
to see, and I'm mighty proud to be associated with folks

513
00:29:19,440 --> 00:29:20,240
like them.

514
00:29:36,460 --> 00:29:41,600
It takes over 3.8 million pounds of highly explosive rocket fuel

515
00:29:41,700 --> 00:29:45,060
to lift the space shuttle and her crew into orbit.

516
00:29:45,061 --> 00:29:46,700
But once they've reached the heavens,

517
00:29:46,800 --> 00:29:51,300
the astronauts themselves are fueled by sterilized, vacuum

518
00:29:51,400 --> 00:29:57,000
sealed, vitamin enriched, reconstituted space food.

519
00:29:57,100 --> 00:29:59,300
The cuisine aboard the shuttle has evolved

520
00:29:59,400 --> 00:30:03,220
from over three decades of research and careful planning.

521
00:30:03,320 --> 00:30:05,420
When America's space program began,

522
00:30:05,520 --> 00:30:08,620
scientists weren't positive that human beings could actually

523
00:30:08,720 --> 00:30:10,620
eat in space.

524
00:30:10,720 --> 00:30:12,560
They questioned an astronaut's ability

525
00:30:12,561 --> 00:30:14,960
to swallow in zero gravity.

526
00:30:15,060 --> 00:30:17,960
Perhaps food would become suspended in the windpipe

527
00:30:18,060 --> 00:30:20,800
and cause suffocation.

528
00:30:20,900 --> 00:30:24,100
In 1962, John Glenn became the first American

529
00:30:24,200 --> 00:30:26,000
to address the problem.

530
00:30:26,100 --> 00:30:27,680
On board his Mercury spacecraft, he

531
00:30:27,780 --> 00:30:31,420
took the first tentative bites of freeze-dried snacks,

532
00:30:31,520 --> 00:30:34,020
served up in squeezable tubes.

533
00:30:34,120 --> 00:30:38,120
The experiment was a success, even if it was only nominally

534
00:30:38,220 --> 00:30:39,020
appetizing.

535
00:30:42,560 --> 00:30:44,900
The Gemini flights in the mid-1960s

536
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:48,060
engaged pairs of astronauts on longer and more complex

537
00:30:48,160 --> 00:30:51,740
missions, requiring larger food packs versus squeeze tube

538
00:30:51,840 --> 00:30:53,100
snacks.

539
00:30:53,200 --> 00:30:56,000
The major Gemini innovation was reconstituting

540
00:30:56,100 --> 00:30:57,300
dehydrated meals.

541
00:31:01,320 --> 00:31:04,320
Apollo missions to the moon introduced the availability

542
00:31:04,420 --> 00:31:09,180
of hot water for warm meals, with meats and sauces prepared

543
00:31:09,280 --> 00:31:11,120
in aluminum foil containers.

544
00:31:12,560 --> 00:31:17,000
Skylab became the pinnacle of space gastronomics.

545
00:31:17,100 --> 00:31:19,200
Because of mission length, more attention

546
00:31:19,300 --> 00:31:21,300
was given to variety.

547
00:31:21,400 --> 00:31:25,500
A special dining area services three astronauts at once,

548
00:31:25,600 --> 00:31:29,880
with a hot plate to keep food warm and storage for frozen

549
00:31:29,980 --> 00:31:32,080
foods like ice cream and steak.

550
00:31:34,940 --> 00:31:38,120
During the Skylab Soyuz mission of 1975,

551
00:31:38,121 --> 00:31:42,920
In 1975, Russia and America had an exchange of cuisine

552
00:31:43,020 --> 00:31:44,820
as well as science.

553
00:31:44,920 --> 00:31:50,360
Cosmonauts serve tubes of borscht to the American astronauts.

554
00:31:50,460 --> 00:31:52,360
We carry all the food we need into orbit with us.

555
00:31:52,460 --> 00:31:54,880
A lot of our food is just like the food you'd eat at home.

556
00:31:54,980 --> 00:31:57,780
Bread, tuna fish, peanuts, cookies.

557
00:31:57,880 --> 00:32:01,180
Now, first, the orange drink.

558
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:08,120
We go over to the water dispenser, put it in, close it up.

559
00:32:08,121 --> 00:32:12,160
And really, all that did is put a needle into the container.

560
00:32:12,161 --> 00:32:13,260
We choose the cold water.

561
00:32:19,100 --> 00:32:21,940
And that rehydrates the drink.

562
00:32:21,941 --> 00:32:25,240
Need to shake it around just a little bit.

563
00:32:25,241 --> 00:32:26,980
That's the orange drink.

564
00:32:26,981 --> 00:32:30,040
For the frankfurters, really all we need to do to that

565
00:32:30,041 --> 00:32:31,540
is just put them in the oven.

566
00:32:31,541 --> 00:32:34,080
This is our oven up here.

567
00:32:34,081 --> 00:32:35,740
Just slip them right in.

568
00:32:35,741 --> 00:32:36,480
Let them heat up.

569
00:32:38,120 --> 00:32:42,360
On the space shuttle, mealtime can have a surreal tone.

570
00:32:42,460 --> 00:32:45,420
Astronauts often eat hanging from the ceiling

571
00:32:45,520 --> 00:32:47,760
or sitting on the floor.

572
00:32:47,860 --> 00:32:51,360
And plates aren't always needed as long as floating food

573
00:32:51,460 --> 00:32:53,560
is caught before it drifts away.

574
00:32:55,780 --> 00:32:58,600
Rounding up a renegade M&M candy soon

575
00:32:58,700 --> 00:33:01,680
becomes second nature for shuttle passengers.

576
00:33:01,780 --> 00:33:06,240
But very small pieces of unsupervised zero-gravity food

577
00:33:06,241 --> 00:33:10,740
could pose a major problem and put an entire mission at risk.

578
00:33:12,920 --> 00:33:15,480
That's why astronauts use liquid salt, which

579
00:33:15,580 --> 00:33:18,980
is mixed into their food while it's still in a sealed pack.

580
00:33:19,080 --> 00:33:22,620
Otherwise, individual grains would float around the cabin,

581
00:33:22,720 --> 00:33:25,220
harming the machinery, the electrical circuits,

582
00:33:25,320 --> 00:33:27,000
or be accidentally inhaled.

583
00:33:29,600 --> 00:33:32,800
An astronaut's main eating utensil is a pair of scissors.

584
00:33:32,900 --> 00:33:36,240
But once a package is snipped, a spoon can be used.

585
00:33:36,241 --> 00:33:40,780
Food is designed to be sticky so it adheres to Earth utensils.

586
00:33:40,880 --> 00:33:44,480
This also allows it to be flung with great accuracy

587
00:33:44,580 --> 00:33:49,480
through the spacecraft or sucked from spinning silverware.

588
00:33:49,580 --> 00:33:52,060
Sometimes even liquids can be consumed

589
00:33:52,160 --> 00:33:54,960
without utensils or containers.

590
00:34:06,240 --> 00:34:10,140
Just as eating takes on new dimensions in space,

591
00:34:10,240 --> 00:34:13,020
so does sleep.

592
00:34:13,120 --> 00:34:16,880
Initially, scientists wondered how weightlessness would affect

593
00:34:16,980 --> 00:34:19,420
an astronaut's ability to sleep.

594
00:34:19,520 --> 00:34:22,560
Perhaps this strange environment would inspire bizarre

595
00:34:22,660 --> 00:34:25,220
nightmares, cause sleep disorders,

596
00:34:25,320 --> 00:34:29,100
and put the entire space program in jeopardy.

597
00:34:29,200 --> 00:34:31,040
The reverse is true.

598
00:34:31,140 --> 00:34:36,240
Crew members experience a unique zero-gravity relaxation.

599
00:34:36,241 --> 00:34:39,040
In the weightlessness of space, sleeping bodies

600
00:34:39,140 --> 00:34:41,480
tend to adopt a fetal position.

601
00:34:41,580 --> 00:34:44,180
Arms float out at shoulder level.

602
00:34:44,280 --> 00:34:47,180
Knees bend.

603
00:34:47,280 --> 00:34:50,880
On the space shuttle, sunrise happens every 90 minutes.

604
00:34:50,980 --> 00:34:54,300
Astronauts often sleep wearing eye masks.

605
00:34:54,400 --> 00:34:58,760
When it came time to go to bed, I'd gather up the liner,

606
00:34:58,860 --> 00:35:03,560
my sleep mask, and my music recorder,

607
00:35:03,561 --> 00:35:06,320
and carry them up with me to the flight deck.

608
00:35:08,260 --> 00:35:10,860
When I got to the flight deck, I'd crawl into the blue bag,

609
00:35:11,440 --> 00:35:14,440
float just above the seat, between the seat and the ceiling,

610
00:35:14,720 --> 00:35:16,060
and right next to the windows.

611
00:35:16,760 --> 00:35:19,600
And when it was time to go to bed, I'd put on the sleep mask,

612
00:35:20,700 --> 00:35:23,840
put on the headphones to hear some music,

613
00:35:24,880 --> 00:35:26,380
try to get comfortable and go to sleep.

614
00:35:26,381 --> 00:35:31,380
Mission 51L, the 25th flight of the space shuttle program,

615
00:35:31,480 --> 00:35:37,420
began at 1138 AM Eastern Standard Time on January 20th, 1986.

616
00:35:37,520 --> 00:35:42,060
It ended 73 seconds later in a structural break

617
00:35:42,160 --> 00:35:46,640
between the main fuel tanks and the Challenger orbiter.

618
00:35:46,740 --> 00:35:48,760
Relatively few people saw the accident

619
00:35:48,860 --> 00:35:51,080
in which seven crew members perished

620
00:35:51,180 --> 00:35:54,280
when it was broadcast live on national television.

621
00:35:54,281 --> 00:35:57,380
when it was broadcast live on national television.

622
00:35:57,480 --> 00:36:01,880
By 1986, the public had been lulled into benign disinterest

623
00:36:01,980 --> 00:36:04,660
due to the cool efficiency of NASA

624
00:36:04,760 --> 00:36:06,500
and the routine of shuttle missions that

625
00:36:06,600 --> 00:36:11,000
disguised the disturbing reality that space flight is dangerous.

626
00:36:16,400 --> 00:36:19,380
Impatience had grown at the launch site.

627
00:36:19,480 --> 00:36:21,680
The Challenger had been fueled and was waiting

628
00:36:21,780 --> 00:36:24,080
for favorable weather conditions.

629
00:36:24,081 --> 00:36:28,120
A long, delayed blast-off was finally set for a chilly Tuesday

630
00:36:28,220 --> 00:36:31,420
morning, even though overnight, ice had accumulated

631
00:36:31,520 --> 00:36:32,320
on the launch pad.

632
00:36:35,220 --> 00:36:38,820
The previous evening, several operational water systems

633
00:36:38,920 --> 00:36:42,100
had been opened up and allowed to flow into drains.

634
00:36:42,200 --> 00:36:46,240
But those drains froze up and caused overflows.

635
00:36:46,340 --> 00:36:51,480
Air temperature at launch was 36 degrees, 15 degrees colder

636
00:36:51,580 --> 00:36:54,080
than any previous attempt.

637
00:36:54,081 --> 00:36:57,580
T-minus 7 minutes 30 seconds.

638
00:36:57,680 --> 00:36:59,660
The ground launch sequencer begins

639
00:36:59,760 --> 00:37:02,080
retracting the crew access arm.

640
00:37:02,180 --> 00:37:04,720
And the last emergency evacuation exit

641
00:37:04,820 --> 00:37:08,700
for the seven astronauts pulls away.

642
00:37:08,800 --> 00:37:11,460
T-minus 3 minutes 15 seconds.

643
00:37:11,560 --> 00:37:14,340
The rotating gimbals of the orbiter's three main engines

644
00:37:14,440 --> 00:37:18,500
are checked, then returned to start positions.

645
00:37:18,600 --> 00:37:20,880
T-minus 3 minutes 15 seconds.

646
00:37:20,881 --> 00:37:24,220
T-minus 2 minutes 50 seconds.

647
00:37:24,320 --> 00:37:28,080
Retraction of the gaseous oxygen vent hood.

648
00:37:30,180 --> 00:37:32,120
T-minus 16 seconds.

649
00:37:32,220 --> 00:37:37,640
Sound suppression water sprayed to absorb engine vibrations.

650
00:37:37,740 --> 00:37:42,240
Hydrogen igniters fire to burn off residual chemical vapors.

651
00:37:42,340 --> 00:37:44,540
T-minus 6.6 seconds.

652
00:37:44,640 --> 00:37:47,380
Main engines ignited in sequence.

653
00:37:47,480 --> 00:37:49,380
Thrusters are burning.

654
00:37:49,381 --> 00:37:52,320
Thrust from the rockets bend the shuttle stack.

655
00:37:52,420 --> 00:37:54,760
When it returns to full vertical position,

656
00:37:54,860 --> 00:37:56,860
solid rocket boosters are ignited.

657
00:38:00,860 --> 00:38:03,500
NASA cameras record smoke emerging

658
00:38:03,600 --> 00:38:07,800
near the connecting ring of the right solid rocket booster.

659
00:38:07,900 --> 00:38:10,000
No one is aware that the cold temperature

660
00:38:10,100 --> 00:38:14,240
has compromised the seal between two booster segments,

661
00:38:14,340 --> 00:38:16,480
and that a searing hot flare is eating

662
00:38:16,580 --> 00:38:18,100
through the thin metal casing which

663
00:38:18,101 --> 00:38:21,700
which holds millions of pounds of the most powerful rocket fuel

664
00:38:21,800 --> 00:38:22,600
ever blended.

665
00:38:25,900 --> 00:38:29,140
Subtle mission, and it has cleared the tower.

666
00:38:29,240 --> 00:38:31,240
7.74 seconds.

667
00:38:31,340 --> 00:38:33,240
The roll maneuver is initiated.

668
00:38:35,460 --> 00:38:38,460
Special long-range cameras capture the side flare

669
00:38:38,560 --> 00:38:39,860
growing ominously.

670
00:38:41,760 --> 00:38:44,260
Velocity 2,257 feet per second.

671
00:38:44,360 --> 00:38:46,200
Altitude 4.3 nautical miles.

672
00:38:48,100 --> 00:38:50,640
Inside the main fuel tank, the liquid propellants

673
00:38:50,641 --> 00:38:55,340
spark an uncontrollable chemical reaction.

674
00:38:55,341 --> 00:38:57,000
Challenger, go with throttle up.

675
00:39:02,740 --> 00:39:05,720
1 minute 15 seconds, velocity 2,900 feet per second,

676
00:39:05,721 --> 00:39:08,380
altitude 9 nautical miles, downrange distance 7 nautical

677
00:39:08,381 --> 00:39:09,081
miles.

678
00:39:09,082 --> 00:39:15,680
The solid rocket boosters revolve in a chaotic spiral

679
00:39:15,780 --> 00:39:20,720
until a rain safety officer initiates their remote control

680
00:39:20,820 --> 00:39:25,400
destruction 110 seconds after launch.

681
00:39:25,500 --> 00:39:29,260
The Challenger orbiter itself is not destroyed by the explosion,

682
00:39:29,360 --> 00:39:33,000
but rather by the erratic aerodynamic effects on the ship

683
00:39:33,100 --> 00:39:35,180
as it careens out of control.

684
00:39:35,181 --> 00:39:38,420
out of control.

685
00:39:38,520 --> 00:39:41,760
The crew cabin remains intact and briefly emerges

686
00:39:41,860 --> 00:39:44,260
from the fireball before its plummet to the sea.

687
00:39:47,360 --> 00:39:50,720
A large-scale search was initiated to recover the debris.

688
00:39:50,820 --> 00:39:56,760
22 ships, six underwater vessels, 33 aircraft.

689
00:39:56,860 --> 00:40:00,780
Those pieces found first were those floating on the surface.

690
00:40:00,880 --> 00:40:04,300
Specially equipped submarines searched for the rest.

691
00:40:04,301 --> 00:40:06,700
Half of the Challenger was recovered in the effort.

692
00:40:09,740 --> 00:40:14,840
93,000 square miles of ocean were searched at depths of 1,200 feet.

693
00:40:14,841 --> 00:40:17,080
Pieces were sent to the logistics facility,

694
00:40:17,081 --> 00:40:20,540
where they were arranged on the floor in their original positions

695
00:40:20,541 --> 00:40:21,241
on the shuttle.

696
00:40:25,020 --> 00:40:27,900
An investigation revealed that the flexing of a joint

697
00:40:27,901 --> 00:40:31,260
on the solid rocket booster created a gap where two rubber

698
00:40:31,261 --> 00:40:33,760
seals held the explosive contents inside.

699
00:40:37,760 --> 00:40:40,600
Film records taken during the assembly of the boosters

700
00:40:40,700 --> 00:40:43,500
were reviewed for more in-depth analysis.

701
00:40:43,600 --> 00:40:46,100
But the photographs showed that the O-rings themselves

702
00:40:46,200 --> 00:40:47,700
were not defective.

703
00:40:47,800 --> 00:40:51,120
The design itself was at fault.

704
00:40:51,220 --> 00:40:54,020
The use of these rubber O-rings and their sensitivity

705
00:40:54,120 --> 00:40:56,260
to low temperature and high stress

706
00:40:56,360 --> 00:41:00,260
was responsible for the accident.

707
00:41:00,261 --> 00:41:02,400
In the haunting silence which followed,

708
00:41:02,500 --> 00:41:08,000
America came to reconsider the space program.

709
00:41:08,100 --> 00:41:12,440
The path to new worlds was no longer an idealistic dream.

710
00:41:12,540 --> 00:41:15,580
In the aftermath of tragedy, the journey into space

711
00:41:15,680 --> 00:41:18,020
could clearly be seen as a profound evolution

712
00:41:18,120 --> 00:41:19,620
of the human animal.

713
00:41:19,720 --> 00:41:23,120
And like all births, there would be pain and sacrifice

714
00:41:23,220 --> 00:41:24,820
to be endured in the process.

715
00:41:24,821 --> 00:41:29,820
America understood that it possessed the technical capability

716
00:41:29,920 --> 00:41:32,320
to put the pieces of its shattered spirit together

717
00:41:32,420 --> 00:41:36,060
and soar back into the heavens.

718
00:41:36,160 --> 00:41:41,060
But would the nation have the heart to continue the quest?

719
00:41:49,080 --> 00:41:50,920
We all live in space.

720
00:41:50,921 --> 00:41:54,720
Like the shuttle, Earth itself is a large craft hurtling

721
00:41:54,820 --> 00:41:59,660
through the cosmos carrying a fragile cargo of life.

722
00:41:59,760 --> 00:42:01,700
Half a century ago, Chuck Yeager

723
00:42:01,800 --> 00:42:03,400
was strapped in a primitive rocket

724
00:42:03,500 --> 00:42:05,700
and became the first man to travel faster

725
00:42:05,800 --> 00:42:08,040
than the speed of sound.

726
00:42:08,140 --> 00:42:11,300
Tomorrow, NASA will introduce a new horizontal approach

727
00:42:11,400 --> 00:42:14,440
to space travel, which no longer risks lives

728
00:42:14,540 --> 00:42:17,640
with the volatility of blastoff.

729
00:42:17,740 --> 00:42:20,820
But today, the space shuttle has rebounded from the train

730
00:42:20,821 --> 00:42:23,820
of the Challenger to become a constant and reliable

731
00:42:23,920 --> 00:42:25,560
technological workhorse.

732
00:42:28,060 --> 00:42:29,820
Discoveries made on shuttle missions

733
00:42:29,920 --> 00:42:33,260
have improved such earthly notions as food processing,

734
00:42:33,360 --> 00:42:38,400
energy systems, emergency medical care, and more.

735
00:42:38,500 --> 00:42:40,480
Perhaps the true value of the shuttle

736
00:42:40,580 --> 00:42:45,780
is that it allows us to look inside ourselves.

737
00:42:45,880 --> 00:42:48,420
From the great height of space, we're

738
00:42:48,421 --> 00:42:51,320
We're given a perspective that transcends the routine

739
00:42:51,420 --> 00:42:56,760
of our daily lives and focuses instead upon our spirit.

740
00:42:56,860 --> 00:42:59,160
We thrive on possibility.

741
00:42:59,260 --> 00:43:02,160
Curiosity is the food of our soul.

742
00:43:02,260 --> 00:43:04,260
There is promise in the heavens.

743
00:43:08,280 --> 00:43:10,180
Today, the space shuttle has become

744
00:43:10,280 --> 00:43:12,280
both a symbol of triumph and tragedy.

745
00:43:12,380 --> 00:43:15,720
Flying in 115 missions, the shuttle's success

746
00:43:15,820 --> 00:43:18,420
has made space travel seem somewhat routine.

747
00:43:18,421 --> 00:43:26,420
But the losses of the Challenger and Columbia have served as a harsh reminder that in fact nothing about space travel is routine.

748
00:43:26,421 --> 00:43:33,420
Even still, the space program continues, paving the way back to the moon, onto Mars, and further into the universe.

749
00:43:33,421 --> 00:43:36,420
For the History Channel, I'm Story Musgrave.

750
00:43:36,421 --> 00:43:38,120
Grave, thanks for watching.

